  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 249 PM CDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\nSouthwesterly flow aloft continues across the Northern Plains as\na warm front moves through eastern Montana into North Dakota \nlater this evening. Storms could fire along the front this \nevening as ample instability remains in place across eastern \nMontana into western North Dakota. However, forecast soundings \nshow that there will be a very strong CAP the further east you \ngo into North Dakota later this evening mainly impacting\nanything that wants to move in the south central. Some of the \nCAMs try to bring a stronger storm across the southwest this \nevening and a cluster of weaker storms across the northwest \nalong the front. The southwest storms are less in coverage now \nas the models have pulled back slightly. CAMs are increasing \ntheir coverage of storms developing in the early morning hours \nacross the north central. SBCAPE tomorrow morning and afternoon \nwill range around 1500-2500 j/kg of cape and 40 kts of shear. \nStorms will likely progress and strengthen by the early \nafternoon across the north central moving across eastern North \nDakota into western Minnesota.\n\nHigh temperatures this afternoon are forecast to warm into the\n80s across the state. Overnight low temperatures tonight are \nforecast to drop down into the upper 50s to lower 60s. A weak \nlow level jet will continue tonight moving eastward with the \nfrontal progression. This could lead to breezy conditions and\nsustained storm development tonight across eastern and central \nNorth Dakota. Tomorrow, temperatures are forecast to warm into \nthe warm upper 70s north to the upper 80s south. The warmer \nweather will move into the region by the end of the week as an \nupper level ridge builds across the region. Temperatures, Friday\nare forecast to warm into the upper 80s to lower 90s. \n\nThe upper level ridge is forecast to break down by this weekend \nleading to cooler temperatures. The ridge breaking down will \nopen the door for an upper level low to move through the region \nSunday into Memorial Day bringing forth increased chances of \nrain. The probabilities continue to increase for 1 inch of rain \nwhere the north now has around 50 percent shot of an inch of \nrain. The NBM 25th to 75th percentile for KBIS are around the \n70s for later next week as the ridge breaks down.",
